1. TerraSentia (EarthSense)

Link: https://www.earthsense.co/terrasentia/

Developers

Developed by EarthSense
Founded by Chinmay Soman and Girish Chowdhary
Originated from University of Illinois research


Design Approach

TerraSentia is designed as a narrow under-canopy robot (~32 cm width) to move between tightly spaced crop rows.
The design focuses on collecting plant-level data beneath foliage, which aerial systems cannot access.
The lightweight chassis reduces soil compaction while maintaining mobility on uneven terrain.


Methods (Technologies & How They Work)
	•	Computer Vision (OpenCV + CNN models):
Uses RGB cameras with LED lighting to capture consistent images.
Images are processed using OpenCV libraries and Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to extract plant features like leaf area and stem width.
	•	LiDAR (Light Detection and Ranging):
Emits laser pulses and measures return time to calculate distance.
Used to generate 3D maps of crops and detect obstacles.
	•	Sensor Fusion Algorithm:
Combines data from:
	•	Camera (visual features)
	•	LiDAR (depth information)
	•	GPS (global position)
This improves accuracy and reduces error from individual sensors.


Implementation Strategy
	•	Navigation System:
Uses GPS for global positioning.
Uses a vanishing point algorithm (image processing technique) to detect crop row alignment and stay centered.
	•	Locomotion System:
Uses 4-wheel independent drive (each wheel has its own motor), improving traction on muddy or uneven terrain.
	•	Processing Architecture:
High-level processor handles AI and vision.
Low-level controllers handle motors and sensors.


Relevance to Our Project

This validates our use of camera-based analysis and sensor fusion.
It supports our decision to use 4WD for rough terrain.
It also confirms the importance of separating high-level and low-level control (Pi + Arduino).


2. BoniRob (Bosch / DeepField Robotics)

Link: https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.ai-for-sdgs.academy/case/311&sa=U&sqi=2&ved=2ahUKEwiTq7PorbGTAxUWaEEAHRyOFcAQFnoECCMQAQ&usg=AOvVaw0IVOm2Yfjm0V1uI0cyXWRg

Developers

Developed by Bosch and DeepField Robotics


Design Approach

BoniRob is a large modular agricultural robot designed as a multi-purpose platform.
It uses a tool-based system where different agricultural modules can be attached.


Methods (Technologies & How They Work)
	•	Image Recognition (Machine Learning):
Uses cameras to capture crop images.
Applies classification algorithms to distinguish weeds from crops based on shape, color, and texture.
	•	Mechanical Weeding System:
Uses an actuator (stamping mechanism) to physically destroy weeds.
This replaces chemical herbicides.
	•	Soil Sensors:
Measures soil parameters like moisture and nutrients using embedded probes.


Implementation Strategy
	•	Dual-Controller Architecture:
High-level controller → navigation and planning
Low-level controller → actuators and sensors
	•	Navigation:
Uses GPS and onboard sensors to move autonomously across the farm.


Relevance to Our Project

This strongly supports our dual-controller system design.
It shows how separating logic improves system reliability.
We apply the same concept using Raspberry Pi and Arduino.


3. Ladybird Robot (University of Sydney)

Link: https://www.google.com/url?q=https://robohub.org/robots-ladybird/&sa=U&sqi=2&ved=2ahUKEwijkLDRrbGTAxXBhv0HHauBAIYQFnoECB4QAQ&usg=AOvVaw1aDCqDHPPEZieyOuh4prM9

Developers

Developed by University of Sydney
Australian Centre for Field Robotics


Design Approach

Ladybird is a solar-powered robot designed for long-term monitoring in vegetable farms.
It focuses on sustainability and continuous operation.


Methods (Technologies & How They Work)
	•	Computer Vision (Image Processing Algorithms):
Uses high-resolution cameras to capture plant images.
Applies segmentation and classification algorithms to detect plant health and weeds.
	•	LiDAR Mapping:
Generates 3D maps of the environment for navigation.
	•	Environmental Sensors:
Measures temperature and humidity using digital sensors.


Implementation Strategy
	•	Autonomous Navigation:
Combines LiDAR and vision to follow crop rows.
	•	Energy System:
Uses solar panels for continuous operation.
	•	Data System:
Collects and stores environmental and crop data for analysis.


Relevance to Our Project

It shows how multiple sensors can be integrated into one system.
It supports our approach of combining soil + environmental + vision data.
It highlights automation in agriculture.


4. FarmDroid FD20

Link: https://farmdroid.com/

Developers

Developed by FarmDroid (Denmark)


Design Approach

FarmDroid is designed as a fully solar-powered robot for precision farming.
It focuses on energy efficiency and long-term operation.


Methods (Technologies & How They Work)
	•	RTK-GPS (Real-Time Kinematic GPS):
Provides centimeter-level accuracy by using correction signals from a base station.
	•	Coordinate-Based Weeding:
Records exact seed positions.
Uses stored coordinates to remove weeds without cameras.


Implementation Strategy
	•	Uses GPS for navigation and positioning
	•	Operates autonomously across the farm
	•	Powered entirely by solar panels


Relevance to Our Project

It shows an alternative to vision-based systems.
It highlights the importance of accurate positioning.
It helps us compare GPS vs camera-based approaches.


5. Thorvald (Saga Robotics)

Link: https://www.sagarobotics.com/thorvald-platform/

Developers

Developed by Saga Robotics
Originated from Norwegian agricultural robotics research


Design Approach

Thorvald is a modular robot that can be adjusted for different farm environments.
Its structure can be reconfigured depending on crop type.


Methods (Technologies & How They Work)
	•	UV-C Light Treatment:
Uses ultraviolet light to kill fungal diseases by damaging pathogen DNA.
	•	ROS (Robot Operating System):
Software framework used to manage sensors, control, and communication.
	•	Distributed Motor Control:
Each wheel has its own motor controller for precise movement.


Implementation Strategy
	•	Operates mainly at night to apply UV treatment
	•	Uses ROS for system coordination
	•	Modular design allows flexibility


Relevance to Our Project

It shows the importance of modular and scalable design.
It introduces advanced applications like disease control.
It inspires flexibility in our system architecture.


